== Chapter 3

#align(center)[#smallcaps(all: true)[THE KNOWLEDGE OF GOD NATURALLY IMPLANTED IN THE HUMAN MIND.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. The knowledge of God, being shown to all, leaves the reprobate without excuse. The universal belief in and acknowledgment of the existence of God.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. Objection—that religion and the belief in a Deity are the inventions of crafty statesmen. Refutation of the objection. This universal belief confirmed by the examples of wicked men and atheists.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. Confirmed also by the vain efforts of the wicked to banish all fear of God from their minds. Conclusion: the knowledge of God is naturally implanted in the human mind.]

#v(0.8em)

1\. #smallcaps[That] there is in the human mind, and indeed by natural instinct, some sense of Deity, we hold to be beyond dispute. For God himself, to prevent any man from pretending ignorance, has given all men some idea of his Godhead, and he constantly renews its memory, again and again instilling fresh drops, so that all, to a man, knowing that there is a God and that he is their Maker, may be condemned by their own conscience when they neither worship him nor consecrate their lives to his service. Certainly, if there is anywhere that God might be supposed to be unknown, the likeliest place to find an example is among the dullest peoples, farthest removed from civilization. But, as a heathen tells us,#footnote[“Intelligi necesse est deos, quoniam insitas eorum vel potius innatas cognitiones habemus.—Quae nobis natura informationem deorum ipsorum dedit, eadem insculpsit in mentibus ut eos aeternos et beatos haberemus.” \[It must be understood that there are gods, since we have implanted, or rather inborn, notions of them.—The same nature that gave us a notion of the gods themselves has engraved it on our minds that we should hold them eternal and blessed.\]—Cic. de Nat. Deor. lib. 1 c. 17.—“Itaque inter omnes omnium gentium summa constat; omnibus enim innatum est, et in animo quasi insculptum esse deos.” \[So the main point is agreed among all men of all nations; for it is inborn in all, and as it were engraved on the mind, that there are gods.\]—Lib. 2. c. 4. See also Lact. Inst. Div. lib. 3 c. 10. (Beveridge’s note.)] there is no nation so barbarous, no race so savage, as not to be filled with the conviction that there is a God. Even those who in other parts of life seem to differ least from the lower animals always retain some seed of religion; so deeply has this common conviction taken hold of the mind, so firmly is it stamped on the hearts of all men. Since, then, there has never been from the very beginning any region of the globe, any city, even any household, without religion, this amounts to a tacit confession that a sense of Deity is inscribed on every heart. Indeed, even idolatry is ample evidence of this. For we know how unwilling man is to lower himself so as to set other creatures above himself. So when he chooses to worship wood and stone rather than be thought to have no God, it is plain how very strong this imprint of a Deity must be, since it is harder to erase it from the mind of man than to break down the feelings of his nature. And these are certainly broken down when, against his natural haughtiness, he willingly humbles himself before the lowliest things as an act of reverence to God.

2\. It is utterly absurd, therefore, to claim, as some do, that religion was invented by the cunning and craft of a few men as a means of keeping the mass of the people in due subjection, while there was nothing that those very men, who taught others to worship God, believed less than that there is a God. I readily admit that scheming men have brought a vast number of fictions into religion in order to fill the common people with reverence or strike them with terror, and so make them more submissive; but they could never have succeeded in this if men’s minds had not already been filled with that firm belief in God from which, as from its seed, the inclination to religion springs. And it is quite incredible that those who cunningly deceived their simpler neighbors in the matter of religion were themselves wholly without any knowledge of God. For though in ancient times there were some, and today not a few are found#footnote[On some atheists of the author’s own time, see Calvinus De Scandalis. (Beveridge’s note.)] who deny that there is a God, yet, whether they like it or not, they now and then feel the truth they wish not to know. We read of no man who broke out into more unbridled and brazen contempt of the Deity than C. Caligula,#footnote[Suet. Calig. c. 51. (Beveridge’s note.)] and yet no one showed greater dread when any sign of divine wrath appeared. So, however unwillingly, he shook with terror before the God whom he openly made it his business to despise. You may see the same thing happen every day to his modern imitators. The boldest despiser of God is the most easily disturbed, trembling at the sound of a falling leaf. Why is this, unless it comes from the vengeance of the divine majesty, which strikes their consciences the harder the more they try to flee from it? They all, indeed, look about for hiding places where they may hide themselves from the presence of the Lord and again wipe it from their minds; but for all their efforts they remain caught in the net. Though the conviction may at times seem to vanish for a moment, it immediately returns and rushes in with new force, so that any break they have from the gnawing of conscience is much like the sleep of drunkards or madmen, who find no quiet rest even in sleep, but are continually haunted by dreadful and horrifying dreams. So even the wicked themselves are an example of the fact that some idea of God always exists in every human mind.

3\. All men of sound judgment will therefore hold that a sense of Deity is indelibly engraved on the human heart. And that this belief is naturally born in all, and fixed deep, as it were, in our very bones, is strikingly proved by the stubbornness of the wicked, who, though they struggle furiously, cannot free themselves from the fear of God. Though Diagoras,#footnote[Cic. De Nat. Deor. lib. 1 c. 23. Valer. Max. lib. 1. c. 1. (Beveridge’s note.)] and others like him, make fun of whatever has been believed about religion in all ages, and Dionysius scoffs at the judgment of heaven, it is only a sardonic grin; for the worm of conscience, sharper than burning steel, is gnawing at them within. I do not say with Cicero that errors wear away with age and that religion grows and improves day by day. For the world (as we will shortly see) labors as hard as it can to shake off all knowledge of God, and corrupts his worship in countless ways. I say only this: though the senseless hardness of heart that the wicked eagerly court as a means of despising God festers in their minds, yet the sense of Deity, which above all things they would like to see extinguished, still thrives and now and then breaks out. From this we conclude that this is not a doctrine first learned in school, but one in which every man is his own teacher from the womb; one which nature herself allows no one to forget, though many strive with all their might to do so. Moreover, if all are born and live for the very purpose of knowing God, and if the knowledge of God, insofar as it falls short of this effect, is fleeting and empty, it is clear that all who do not direct the whole of their thoughts and actions in life to this end degenerate from the law of their creation. Even the philosophers saw this. For this is just what Plato meant (in #emph[Phœd. et Theact.]) when he taught, as he often does, that the chief good of the soul lies in likeness to God, that is, when, by coming to know him, the soul is wholly transformed into him. So Gryllus, too, in Plutarch,#footnote[#emph[Lib. quod bruta anim. ratione utantur.] (Beveridge’s note.)] reasons most skillfully when he declares that if religion is once banished from the lives of men, they not only are in no way superior to the brutes, but are in many ways far more wretched, since, exposed to so many forms of evil, they drag out a troubled and restless existence all their days; and that the only thing, therefore, that makes them superior is the worship of God, through which alone they aspire to immortality.
